course: dictionary
lesson: 15301
title: Context and words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so the context tells you which one is meant. Slag is one of them. In science and geography, slag is the rocky waste left over when metal is taken out of ore, the rock the metal comes from. «Long ago, miners piled the grey slag into a huge slag heap beside the pit.» The words slag heap and piled tell you it is a heap of leftover rock, not something to eat or wear.
explain: Slag is also part of the phrase slag off, which is informal UK slang for criticising someone rudely. «Dad said it was unkind to slag off his friend behind his back.» Behind his back and unkind show you that the words were nasty and said without the friend knowing.
explain: Slag also has a very rude meaning, used as an insult for a woman. It is offensive and hurtful, so never use it or repeat it. If you hear it used, tell a trusted adult.
explain: When you meet a word with more than one meaning, read the sentence and ask which meaning fits. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Slag means the rocky waste left over when metal is taken from ore.
fact: A «slag heap» is a big pile of slag, like the grey heaps left by old coal mines in Wales and northern England.
fact: «Slag off» means to criticise someone rudely, behind their back. It is UK slang.
fact: When you mean the waste material, slag is uncountable: you say some slag, not a slag.
fact: Slag has a very rude insult meaning too. Never use that meaning, and never repeat it.
exercise: «The miners piled the grey waste into a slag heap beside the pit.» What is a slag heap? | A tall pile of hay for the animals | A heap of wet leaves in the park | *A pile of rocky waste left over from mining
exercise: «Nothing grew on the slag heap, because the rock was rough and bare.» What is slag? | Soft, green grass | A kind of wool | *Rocky waste left after metal is taken from rock
exercise: «Dad said it was unkind to slag off his friend behind his back.» What does slag off mean? | To wash a car very carefully | To carry heavy rocks up a hill | *To criticise someone rudely behind their back | To say sorry loudly
test: «Miners left the slag beside the pit, and over many years it grew into a hill.» Where does the slag come from? | A bakery oven | *Waste left over when metal is taken from rock | A pair of old boots | A farm pond
test: Ella says, «Stop slagging off your little brother.» What is she asking you to do? | Wrap up a present | Take a bath | *Stop saying nasty things about him | Carry the rubbish out
exam: Which sentence uses slag with its mining meaning? | «The grey slag sat on the table next to the cups.» | *«The miners tipped the slag down the hillside.» | «He slagged off his best friend for losing the game.»
